    "turn_text": "Yes, the office of speaker does matter. But also, let me just say something. Not everybody. I mean, if in no offense, let me just use this. If I was to say today, they're going to nominate Jenna Ellis for speaker. And you got 218 votes. God help you. Because number one, you're a very bright person, you're very accomplished, very child, but that job is not just the class president. It is the third in line to the presidency. It is over the entire congressional landscape there on Capitol Hill. You've got to negotiate not only your body, but your members, but you've got to negotiate the Democrats as well. You've got to negotiate the Senate. You've got to negotiate the presidency. This, I mean, and just on a simple note, Byron Donalds, who I like a lot, he's been on my show on the Duck Island's podcast, there's been others, I like him a lot. And think he might eventually be a good speaker, but not, I mean, he would have a struggle to get the staffing up, to get the ideas up, to get the things. All of that pushing, by the way, any committee work in the house, any committee work that the Republicans want to do, any legislation, all that's going to be pushed until he can get up and rolling, then you have the steering committee up and rolling. So again, this idea that we can just magically do away with our problems. And you hit something perfect there, and I appreciate it. We're not going to solve these problems overnight. You're not going to get rid of a $3 trillion deficit overnight, although some people promise that. Oh, we're going to cut all this money out of the budget. No, you're not. No, you're not. You know, you're going to have to start taking it in incremental. I'm glad to at least hear both sides talking about growth that can move us toward a project. Maybe that's the best thing that's coming out of this so far. But whoever they get, I mean, I hope they turn out well, but I have to remind her for your listeners. that when John Boehner, which everybody wanted to see gone, when John said he wasn't going to, he had stayed till we got a new speaker, McCarthy was step forward, McCarthy was shot down, a couple others were not nominated, and Paul Ryan, who did not want to be speaker, was main speaker, and all those people who were going to vote against McCarthy voted for Paul Ryan, just a reminder.",
