    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/6/5/65179.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 10,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, and, and he was the worst disaster to happen, in my opinion, of the first two years of the Trump administration installing a lot of that. And so, you know, all of these politics and these practicalities, Doug Collins, I think, are really important to remember, because as much as we can look at Kevin McCarthy and what, you know, some are terming the McLeadership and the establishment and frustration of all of that, It seems to me that it would have been good at post November and seeing that this is possible to object to a speaker to have someone else in the wings who was ready, willing, and able to step forward, unlike Jim Jordan, who doesn't want to be speaker. And I think you're absolutely right. I mean, if I was nominated today, I wouldn't be competent and capable of just walking in there and knowing all of the process and procedures. I mean, there's a reason that you study for some of these things and you get competency on the job. You go and you're part of the Congress for a while before you go into leadership. I mean, that's the practical sum of any job is that you can't just walk into something and think that you're going to know the ropes right away. And so if we look, though, at what the Republicans have promised, in my view, it almost seems like we're doing a page and we're taking a page from the Democrats playbook when they're promising the moon. They're fine with tearing down the Constitution. They're fine with just issuing executive orders that are completely outside the scope of the Constitution to get their outcome-determined solutions, and they're fine with a runaway activist judiciary. But we have forgotten, I think, as a Republican Party and as conservatives what it is that we're conserving. It's actually a good thing that a slight majority of one branch of government can't accomplish everything that they want, because if this were the Democrats, we'd be very happy with that. So we have to return to the understanding of why our founder separated and limited powers, but we also have to return to the understanding, I think, of why leadership matters. And I think that for Kevin McCarthy, you're absolutely right that, you know, he's probably behind the eight ball that he didn't go and broker some of these deals and isn't really acting like a leader at least to some of these 20 who don't have confidence in him. And that should be very concerning for him, not just on a national stage, but if you have a football team or you have any sort of team that there is that huge and vocal of a percentage that doesn't have confidence in your leadership. Then when is the time and when do you say there isn't a pathway and you go back and you conference and you say okay Who can we all get behind when is that moment?",
