    "turn_idx": 11,
    "turn_text": "Well, I think it's interesting you should say that because, I mean, almost in no other place would we say a 90 10, the 10% would show a complete, you know, lack of trust in this leader, although with the 10%, it would be very real with them. Look, what would you call somebody who won an election 90 to 10 and in November the third this year? You'd call it a landslide. That's what you would call it. Now, the realization is that's not the real election. In this case, you had to go to the floor and get to 18. So the real race was to to 18. And I get that. So, but I think that it is an interesting picture here that I don't I will say something. Maybe it's just bold. Maybe it's just an honest. I'm just tend to be honest about this stuff. I don't think he could have got the votes anywhere. I don't think he negotiated anything. I don't think there'd have been five votes there. Again, I believe that the closeness of the majority is what has triggered this from from minute one. and anything less than 10 probably would have done this. But when it got to before, all you needed was five, and I think that happens. Here's the question I think all of us have to understand. The Republicans this year, and that includes all 222 of them, they are responsible for passing 12 appropriations bills. They are not the Democrats. They can't wine, moan, and complain about anybody else. They are. If they cannot put 218 votes together, and I was a part of this in Congress and I yelled about it, screamed about it, was just made fun of because you don't do this, but 12 votes, 12 different votes, and you may not like it. They may not be the bills. But four people get to say, I don't want to, 218 had better pass it. If not, if we get to September, frankly, if we get to the end of July and the Republicans have not passed 12 appropriations bill, then I just frankly would just humbly tell to my colleagues, you need to be quiet. You don't, you don't need to complain about the Senate. You don't need to complain about an omnibus. You don't need to complain about a chronicles because here's the bill for everybody out there who thinks the solution in life is shut down the government. Frankly, you're wrong. Okay, it doesn't fix anything in history has shown us that it front. We're not gaining on that. Number two, if you don't do that, then you send it to the Senate. If the Senate doesn't send it back, then fine, then you've got a leg to say, okay, we've passed these. But at the end of the day, how do we make this better? And that's how you make the argument. But this idea that, you know, we're not going to pass it because we can't. I can't tell you how many times in the last eight years that I served, we couldn't get 218 votes in the majority for bills. And that means you're taking a, and I'll use the example of a Don Bacon in Omaha, Nebraska, which is a Democrat seat and a Jim Jordan in Ohio, they at some point have got to figure out how to get to 218. And that's, you know, sort of the issue we have.",
