    "episode_id": 65179,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/6/5/65179.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 15,
    "turn_text": "I mean, that's gonna be interesting because again, if you say one name, then it goes, well, you're not concerned enough. You're saying nothing. They're not, they're too conservative. And it goes back and forth. I mean, here's the bottom line. We've got to decide if we like a majority that gives speeches or a good majority that legislates. That's your bottom line. And, and I don't care what, how, you know, to me, Jenna in politics, and when you go to make that card and that yes vote, there's, there's a yes or no, and, and, a, there's not a yes, no, and a perfect button. If there was a perfect button, I'd have pushed it every time. There's not a perfect button. I don't care. And they've got to figure this out. And so, I mean, you've got folks from, you know, ranging from one of the, there's a guy who many conservatives wouldn't like at all because he's too practical in many ways. And I agree with this. Tom Cole at Oklahoma is a, is just a rock solid conservative. Now, we may disagree on some of the things that, you know, we would want to do a little bit more here. But I mean, I'm just say, Tom, to use an example, Tom will never get the votes because Tom would be willing to compromise on appropriations bill more than some conservatives would or others. So, but all I'm saying is, at the end of the day, Patrick McHenry, Byron Donald's, Chip Roy, you know, just name your sort of flavor of the",
