  {
    "episode_id": 65179,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/6/5/65179.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 18,
    "turn_text": "I love Mike today. Mike is a great friend. I think Mike could do a good job. But here's the question. At the end of the day, and I think this is the question we're going to have to ask ourselves, is at the end of the day, are we willing to put 218 up for a less than perfect bill? Okay, and live with it to try and get more down the road. Or if Mike Johnson put up an appropriations bill that 10 Republican or 10, you know, conservatives has not concerned enough, then all of a sudden they turn on Mike. Mike becomes a rhino. And that's the problem that you have. We got to quit throwing terms around, you know, loosely, especially with people who've never been in a legislative body. And if you've never had the vote, now, I have great ideas from friends who've never been in legislative, but at the end of the day, it's two 18 or you're making YouTube speeches. I mean, that's the bottom line. And so I think my, I mean, there's there's so a Kelly Armstrong. I mean, there's, I mean, I can name off tons. Greg's to be. I like Greg. I mean, but at the end of the day, though, are you going to let them be a leader? Or is it going to be you're only a leader if you agree with me? And I think that's the part that we're struggling with as a conference right now. Democrats don't have this problem. They fall in line. That's why Pelosi could handle her conference better at five votes than we can, because they do fall in line. One of the things I disagree on, Republicans, we tend to want to get everything or nothing. Democrats will get 1% and come back next year and say, and go back to their voters and say, we succeeded. And Henry Waxman always wanted, I mean, he was the one who actually put through Obamacare if you remember this. Waxman had been in Congress for 40-something years. They put him in control of energy and commerce over Dingle because they wanted to get through the Obamacare bill. And that was a big, if you go back to that hoo-hoo around that time. That was an interesting time. But anyway, put him in there. He had been yelling for universal healthcare for years and years and years and years. But yet he'd go back and say, okay, we raised the eligibility and we got a hundred new members into the Medicare pool. Well, he took that as a victory. He didn't take it as a defeat. He took it as a victory. We've got to get back into that mode of saying, if we're going to make this ship turn, which is so large, we've got to start taking victories. So, you know, look, I don't know who the name will be. There'll be a name at some point. I don't think it's going to be Kevin McCarthy, especially after five votes going down. The only question, you know, frankly, is how long does this last? And here's the problem. There is no House of Representatives until we get a speaker.",
